{
244: {skill_tested: "Identify the economic principle behind pooled operators.", question: "Why could one bureau economically serve many businesses?", option1: "Every client's calls arrived at exactly the same time.", option2: "Intermittent calls from different clients could be handled by a shared continuous staff.", option3: "Operators never needed client-specific instructions.", option4: "Telephone companies prohibited businesses from hiring receptionists.", correctAnswer: "Intermittent calls from different clients could be handled by a shared continuous staff.", explanation: "Step 1: Individual businesses had calls only at intervals.<br><br>Step 2: Calls across many businesses provided steadier work for shared operators.<br><br>Answer: Intermittent calls from different clients could be handled by a shared continuous staff.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
245: {skill_tested: "Identify the function of client cards.", question: "The client cards primarily helped operators:", option1: "repair telephone cables without technicians.", option2: "avoid recording any information from callers.", option3: "apply the correct greeting, contacts, and urgency rules to each line.", option4: "make medical or technical decisions for clients.", correctAnswer: "apply the correct greeting, contacts, and urgency rules to each line.", explanation: "Step 1: Operators shifted rapidly among different businesses.<br><br>Step 2: Cards supplied the identity and approved protocol associated with the ringing line.<br><br>Answer: apply the correct greeting, contacts, and urgency rules to each line.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
246: {skill_tested: "Identify the passage's central historical claim.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "Machines eliminated all work once performed by answering bureaus.", option2: "Human operators succeeded because they ignored written protocols.", option3: "Digital tools restored answering bureaus to their original form.", option4: "Answering services evolved as pooling, automation, and specialization repeatedly redistributed the work of receiving and interpreting calls.", correctAnswer: "Answering services evolved as pooling, automation, and specialization repeatedly redistributed the work of receiving and interpreting calls.", explanation: "Step 1: Bureaus pooled intermittent demand into staffed service.<br><br>Step 2: Machines took over routine capture, while specialized operators retained interpretive tasks and adopted digital tools.<br><br>Answer: Answering services evolved as pooling, automation, and specialization repeatedly redistributed the work of receiving and interpreting calls.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
247: {skill_tested: "Analyze a system-wide scaling risk.", question: "Why could one outdated client card become especially harmful in a larger bureau?", option1: "Many operators might repeatedly apply the same incorrect instructions across calls.", option2: "Large bureaus did not use any client records.", option3: "Callers could edit the physical cards remotely.", option4: "An outdated card prevented every line from ringing.", correctAnswer: "Many operators might repeatedly apply the same incorrect instructions across calls.", explanation: "Step 1: Client cards standardized how operators handled each line.<br><br>Step 2: A larger staff relied on the same shared protocol.<br><br>Step 3: Standardization increases consistency but also spreads an embedded error widely.<br><br>Answer: Many operators might repeatedly apply the same incorrect instructions across calls.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
248: {skill_tested: "Explain bounded professional judgment.", question: "The leaking-pipe and patient examples primarily show that skilled operators had to:", option1: "solve technical and medical problems during the call.", option2: "recognize urgency and escalate appropriately without assuming the client's professional authority.", option3: "refuse all callers who provided incomplete details.", option4: "replace emergency services with written messages.", correctAnswer: "recognize urgency and escalate appropriately without assuming the client's professional authority.", explanation: "Step 1: Important risk may emerge indirectly or through incomplete details.<br><br>Step 2: Operators ask permitted questions and escalate uncertainty.<br><br>Step 3: Their judgment concerns routing and urgency, not diagnosis or repair.<br><br>Answer: recognize urgency and escalate appropriately without assuming the client's professional authority.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
249: {skill_tested: "Analyze how automation redistributes work.", question: "According to the passage, automated menus shifted some work by requiring:", option1: "operators to hand-copy every recorded word immediately.", option2: "clients to predict the exact time of every future call.", option3: "callers to choose categories and employees to sort stored messages later.", option4: "machines to interpret changes in a caller's tone.", correctAnswer: "callers to choose categories and employees to sort stored messages later.", explanation: "Step 1: Machines made capture cheap and abundant.<br><br>Step 2: Menus required callers to navigate predefined choices, and staff later reviewed messages.<br><br>Step 3: Automation relocated sorting rather than removing it completely.<br><br>Answer: callers to choose categories and employees to sort stored messages later.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
250: {skill_tested: "Infer why specialization supported survival.", question: "Specialized bureaus could compete with automated systems chiefly because they:", option1: "stored fewer messages than individual machines.", option2: "charged less than all forms of automated capture.", option3: "eliminated client-specific training and privacy procedures.", option4: "performed time-sensitive interpretation and constrained escalation that recordings handled poorly.", correctAnswer: "performed time-sensitive interpretation and constrained escalation that recordings handled poorly.", explanation: "Step 1: Routine message capture became inexpensive through automation.<br><br>Step 2: Specialized services handled privacy, multilingual reports, hazard grouping, and production urgency.<br><br>Step 3: Their value moved from basic availability to early informed sorting.<br><br>Answer: performed time-sensitive interpretation and constrained escalation that recordings handled poorly.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
251: {skill_tested: "Evaluate continuity amid technological change.", question: "How does the paragraph about digital tools complicate a simple human-versus-machine account?", option1: "It shows that retained human work was itself reorganized by software, scripts, and monitoring.", option2: "It proves that specialized bureaus stopped using operators.", option3: "It shows that digital records eliminated shared errors.", option4: "It argues that human labor remained exactly as it had begun.", correctAnswer: "It shows that retained human work was itself reorganized by software, scripts, and monitoring.", explanation: "Step 1: Humans remain in the specialized bureaus.<br><br>Step 2: Screens, encrypted messages, duplicate detection, detailed scripts, and centralized settings change their work.<br><br>Step 3: Technology and human service become integrated rather than occupying separate historical stages.<br><br>Answer: It shows that retained human work was itself reorganized by software, scripts, and monitoring.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
252: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the persistent problem across industry stages.", question: "The final sentence suggests that every stage of the industry addresses a tension between:", option1: "the cost of radio equipment and the supply of paper message slips.", option2: "the immediate timing of incoming communication and the delayed availability of the responsible person.", option3: "callers who prefer machines and businesses that prohibit telephones.", option4: "professional decisions and the absence of any routing rules.", correctAnswer: "the immediate timing of incoming communication and the delayed availability of the responsible person.", explanation: "Step 1: Early businesses missed calls while workers were away.<br><br>Step 2: Bureaus, recordings, and specialized services each capture or interpret communication before the responsible person responds.<br><br>Step 3: Technology changes the intermediary, but not the mismatch between when a call arrives and when expertise is available.<br><br>Answer: the immediate timing of incoming communication and the delayed availability of the responsible person.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
